    <item>
      <title>New SAF mappings connect Zero Trust objectives to roles and capabilities — 6 November 2026</title>
      <link>https://daily.security.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://daily.security.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 08:15:00 GMT</pubDate>
      <description><![CDATA[<div style="max-width:760px;color:#11181d;font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><div style="margin:0 0 24px"><div style="margin:0 0 6px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.3 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase">Day in brief · 6 November 2026</div><h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;font:650 28px/1.2 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.security.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">New SAF mappings connect Zero Trust objectives to roles and capabilities</a></h2><p style="margin:0;color:#263030;font:16px/1.6 Arial,sans-serif">A new Security standards and frameworks page maps framework objectives to SAF disciplines, roles, Microsoft security capabilities, and implementation guidance, including Zero Trust, the CISA Zero Trust Maturity Model, and the DoD Zero Trust Strategy. The SAF guidance now explains relationships among business priorities, risk, Zero Trust principles, disciplines, technologies, and technical solutions, while adding shared-responsibility, implementation, measurement, and four-component framework guidance. SAF navigation also shifts to objective- and role-based entry points. Most remaining updates refresh publication dates, internal references, or headings across Security Adoption and Zero Trust workshop pages.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.security.admin.news/assets/products/security-adoption.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.security.admin.news/assets/topics/frameworks.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Security adoption · Standards and frameworks</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.security.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#doc-2026-11-06-security-standards-and-frameworks-01" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">Framework guidance maps Zero Trust objectives to SAF implementation paths</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The new page links security standards and framework objectives to SAF disciplines, organizational roles, Microsoft security capabilities, and implementation guidance. Its examples cover Zero Trust, the CISA Zero Trust Maturity Model, and the DoD Zero Trust Strategy.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.security.admin.news/assets/products/security-adoption.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.security.admin.news/assets/topics/frameworks.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Security adoption · Standards and frameworks</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.security.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#doc-2026-11-06-microsoft-security-adoption-framework-saf-04" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">SAF guidance adds shared-responsibility and measurement context</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The updated SAF page connects business priorities, risk, Zero Trust principles, security disciplines, technologies, and technical solutions. It adds guidance on shared responsibilities, implementation, measurement, and four connected framework components across hybrid, multicloud, and multiplatform environments.</p></div><div style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d3c7"><table role="presentation" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" style="width:100%;border:0;border-collapse:collapse"><tr><td width="46" valign="top" style="width:46px;min-width:46px;padding:1px 8px 0 0;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap"><img src="https://daily.security.admin.news/assets/products/security-adoption.svg" width="20" height="20" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:20px!important;max-width:20px!important;height:20px!important;max-height:20px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"><span style="display:inline-block;width:5px"></span><img src="https://daily.security.admin.news/assets/topics/general.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" style="display:inline-block;width:16px!important;max-width:16px!important;height:16px!important;max-height:16px!important;object-fit:contain;vertical-align:middle"></td><td valign="top" style="padding:0;vertical-align:top"><div style="margin:0 0 5px;color:#0d5745;font:700 11px/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Security adoption · General</div><h3 style="margin:0 0 7px;font:600 18px/1.3 Georgia,serif"><a href="https://daily.security.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/#doc-2026-11-06-using-saf-effectively-03" style="color:#11181d;text-decoration:none">SAF navigation now directs readers by objective and organizational role</a></h3></td></tr></table><p style="margin:5px 0 0;color:#46514e;font:14px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif">The retitled “Using SAF effectively” article replaces top-down, build-up, and scenario-driven patterns with tables that route readers to strategy, architecture, disciplines, scenarios, technologies, workshops, and related guidance based on their objectives or roles.</p></div><p style="margin:24px 0 0"><a href="https://daily.security.admin.news/day/2026-11-06/" style="color:#0d5745;font:700 15px/1.4 Georgia,serif">View all updates for 6 November 2026 →</a></p></div>]]></description>
